## Outcome

Generate or import Skim-ready **scenes** and precompute a very short, source-grounded summary for every positively eligible scene before the listener invokes Skim.

The pipeline must make this interaction immediate:

- **Skim Forward:** speak the current scene summary, then begin the next scene.
- **Skim Backward:** speak the previous scene summary, then begin the current scene.

This issue owns scene proposal, summary generation, summarization eligibility, validation, preparation, and evaluation. The portable contract is owned by #88. Playback behavior is tracked separately.

## Product model

A scene is the smallest coherent semantic unit worth navigating. It may be a claim, topic, narrative event, procedural step, or conversational movement. “Scene” remains the product term even when the underlying content is not narrative.

The summary covers the **whole scene**, not merely the unheard suffix. If a listener is 90% through a scene and wants only a small literal movement, they retain separately configurable time-skip controls. Skim is semantic transport, not a replacement for precise time seeking.

The summary should be stylistically reduced while semantically maintaining:

- speaker or claimant when material;
- stance and attribution;
- central claim, event, or step;
- negation, uncertainty, and indispensable qualifiers;
- numbers, prices, dates, and named entities only when confidently grounded.

A candidate target is roughly 2–6 seconds of spoken audio. That is an evaluation target, not permission to remove meaning. If a safe summary is not materially shorter than the scene, the scene may be marked ineligible rather than forced into a misleading compression.

## Typed pipeline

Keep these work types distinct.

### 1. Source normalization and evidence

- stable text ranges, source locators, transcript segments/spans, and media time;
- publisher chapter evidence where available;
- source identity, revision/edition, and attribution;
- confidence and missing-data states;
- internal full native-audio transcripts remain non-user-visible derived data.

### 2. Scene-boundary proposal

Compare and combine:

- authored headings and canonical document navigation;
- publisher podcast chapters;
- deterministic text structure;
- transcript change points or established segmentation methods;
- bounded model assistance where an authorized runtime is available.

Publisher chapters are authoritative authored structure and are never silently replaced. A scene layer may refine within or connect across them only under explicit validation rules.

### 3. Summarization eligibility

Eligibility is not a binary “ad detector.” Require positive evidence that a source span is suitable for semantic summarization.

At minimum distinguish:

- eligible editorial scene;
- excluded/non-scene audio;
- unknown or insufficiently grounded span.

Ads, dynamically inserted promos, music, silence, and other unmapped audio may remain fully playable while absent from the scene tree. Do not require a user-visible “Ad” node. Excluded and unknown spans receive no generated summary.

Absence of ad evidence does not imply editorial eligibility.

### 4. Scene-summary generation

- summarize one bounded scene at a time;
- retain scene/source IDs through every generation step;
- never let a model invent positions or choose recipient-local timestamps;
- validate speaker, polarity, numbers, named entities, quotation, and source coverage in app-owned code where possible;
- retain generation/model/version provenance;
- never present the generated wording as a source quotation.

### 5. Preparation and failure

- scene summaries and bridge audio are prepared before the user presses Skim;
- no demand-time model call may sit on the Skim button path;
- cancellation, thermal/resource pressure, model unavailability, or failure leaves the prior valid map and playback unchanged;
- invalid generated structure must not replace publisher chapters, an existing valid map, or mapless playback;
- the same scene summary is reusable for forward closure and backward context.

## Execution topology

Evaluate each source class independently rather than assuming one model path:

1. imported/hand-authored scene maps;
2. deterministic native-text scenes;
3. eligible on-device summary generation;
4. publisher-chapter podcasts with an adequate transcript;
5. native-audio generation only after #51 satisfies its server-hosted position, latency, privacy, consent, cost, and retention stopping rules.

The earlier on-device podcast-map path was stopped for unacceptable physical-device latency and a media-outside-timeline failure. Do not make this issue a silent revival of that implementation.

## Rights and transport boundary

- #14 governs whether and where AI-derived scene summaries may leave the device.
- #88 carries summary provenance, source attribution, and portable anchor evidence.
- Full generated native-audio transcripts and large source excerpts cannot appear in scene maps, exports, diagnostics, clipboard, search, or share surfaces.
- Semantic faithfulness is required, but it does not by itself establish a right to redistribute a derivative.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] A valid #88 map can be populated from hand-authored fixtures without any model.
- [ ] Representative native text produces ordered, non-overlapping, source-resolvable scenes and concise summaries.
- [ ] Publisher chapters remain intact and authoritative while optional scene refinement remains explicitly distinguishable.
- [ ] Every accepted summary is traceable to exactly one scene and its covered source evidence.
- [ ] Eligibility has explicit eligible, excluded, and unknown outcomes; excluded/unknown spans never receive generated summaries.
- [ ] Ads and other non-scene intervals remain playable without appearing as convenient timeline labels.
- [ ] Summaries preserve speaker attribution, stance, negation, uncertainty, material numbers, and indispensable qualifiers across the evaluation corpus.
- [ ] A summary that cannot safely compress the scene is rejected or marked ineligible rather than forced under the duration target.
- [ ] Skim-ready text and bridge audio are available without a model call at button-press time.
- [ ] Generation failure, cancellation, unavailability, or interruption leaves playback and the previous valid structure unchanged.
- [ ] Internal native-audio transcripts cannot escape through maps, exports, diagnostics, portable packages, clipboard, or search.
- [ ] Contract validators, focused tests, restoration/cancellation tests, and proportional app builds pass.
- [ ] Automated checks establish `verified`; only product-owner listening establishes `human_validated`.

## Evaluation and audit

Use one representative corpus across alternatives:

- heading-rich and heading-free text;
- argument, interview, narrative, and procedure;
- short and long scenes;
- multiple speakers and interruptions;
- noisy transcription;
- negation, hedging, corrections, and disputed claims;
- numbers, prices, dates, and eligibility conditions;
- ads at chapter ends and mid-scene;
- dynamic ad variants, music, silence, and repeated jingles;
- topics that return after long gaps.

Compare:

1. authored/publisher structure with hand-written summaries;
2. deterministic boundaries plus bounded summaries;
3. the selected model-assisted pipeline;
4. mapless/no-Skim fallback.

Record boundary precision/distance, unresolved anchors, summary factual-equivalence review, omission rate, compression ratio, spoken duration, generation latency, energy/thermal state, cancellation responsiveness, and unsupported/failure rate.

Falsification checks must include prompt injection in transcripts, wrong-speaker attribution, reversed stance, removed negation, altered numbers, summarization of an excluded ad, duplicate scenes, out-of-order IDs, process termination, model unavailability, and a changed source revision.

Stop or narrow the feature if it cannot produce fully resolvable scenes, if summaries regularly require source-republishing excerpts, if any excluded/unknown interval is treated as eligible by default, or if representative listeners cannot distinguish generated bridges from source speech.

## Dependencies and related work

- #88 defines the portable scene and summary contract and is a hard dependency.
- #14 owns rights, attribution, and AI-derived sharing policy.
- #46 supplies publisher podcast chapters.
- #51 gates server-hosted native-audio map generation.
- #27 governs later cross-edition placement; exact-edition generation may proceed independently.
- #42 supplies relevant on-device Foundation Models experience but does not authorize full-source podcast map generation.
- #48 is advanced cross-item semantic research and is not required.
- #64 and #73 provide prepared-audio and preparation-scheduling precedents; do not create an unbounded parallel job system.

## Non-goals

- No Skim player controls.
- No public transcript reader.
- No ad chapter labels or ad-summary product.
- No cross-item semantic graph.
- No assumption that every source or device supports generated scenes.
- No authorization to enter an active release lane; implementation requires a later product-owner release-plan update.

